<h1 id="quickstart">Quickstart</h1>
<p>This guide walks through the normal Jundot loop: create a project with AI, refine it, verify it, and only then prepare a GitHub upload.</p>

<h2 id="create-project">1. Create a project with AI</h2>
<p>Describe the game type, theme, controls, and core loop. The AI should summarize the idea and propose an English project name before creating files.</p>

<h2 id="refine-project">2. Refine with the AI</h2>
<p>After the first project is created, continue describing what should change. The AI can edit scenes, scripts, resources, and project settings when the current engine already supports the requirement.</p>

<h2 id="engine-boundary">3. Check the engine boundary</h2>
<p>If the request cannot be completed with the current engine, the AI should explain why. Engine changes should be explicit, scoped, and confirmed by the user before implementation.</p>

<div class="docs-callout">
    <div class="docs-callout-title">Simple rule</div>
    <p>If project files can solve the request, change the project. If engine behavior is missing, request an engine change. If the engine change is generic, prepare it as a main-engine improvement.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="fix-errors">4. Fix errors or performance problems</h2>
<p>When an error or performance problem appears, share the message, logs, or reproduction steps with the AI. The AI can inspect the project, propose a fix, apply it, and ask you to verify the result.</p>

<h2 id="upload">5. Upload after verification</h2>
<p>After you verify the fix or feature in the editor, the AI can prepare a commit and upload the change to GitHub. The commit should include the user-visible result and the checks that passed.</p>
